      <h1 class="blog-post__title">My AI booked a Pilates class for me — because it knows me.</h1>
      <p class="blog-post__subtitle">I had a gap after work on Tuesday. I told my AI, and it booked my favourite class with my preferred instructor — because it remembers my preferences from past bookings.</p>
      <div class="blog-post__meta">
        <span>April 2026</span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>2 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-post__hero-image">
      <img src="/images/blog-pilates-booking.png" alt="AI booking a Pilates class">
    </div>

    <div class="blog-post__content">
      <p>Tuesday afternoon. I finish work and realize I have a gap before dinner. I want to work out, but I don't want to spend 10 minutes scrolling through the Mindbody app checking schedules.</p>

      <p>So I send my AI a message:</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>"I have a gap after work tonight. Book a Pilates class for me."</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>30 seconds later:</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>"Booked ✅ Reformer Pilates with Sabrina at Empower Fitness, 7:15 PM. Added to your calendar. Same studio as last week."</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Why this is different from Siri or Google Assistant</h2>

      <p>Siri can set a reminder. Google Assistant can search the web. But my AI:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Knows my schedule</strong> — it has access to my calendar, so it knows what "after work" means today specifically</li>
        <li><strong>Remembers my preferences</strong> — Reformer Pilates, not mat. Sabrina as instructor. Empower Fitness in Central</li>
        <li><strong>Learns from past bookings</strong> — it's booked classes for me before, so it knows the drill</li>
        <li><strong>Takes action, not just suggests</strong> — it actually books the class and adds it to my calendar, not just tells me where to go</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The difference is context. My AI has been with me long enough to build up a picture of what I like, when I'm free, and how I prefer things done.</p>

      <h2>The memory advantage</h2>

      <p>Every interaction makes it better. Over time, my AI has learned:</p>

      <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>Studio preference:</strong> Empower Fitness, 19/F Li Dong Building, Central</p>
        <p><strong>Class type:</strong> Reformer Pilates</p>
        <p><strong>Preferred instructor:</strong> Sabrina Lui</p>
        <p><strong>Preferred time:</strong> Weekday evenings, 7:00-8:00 PM</p>
        <p><strong>Booking lead time:</strong> Same day is fine, but popular classes fill up by afternoon</p>
      </div>

      <p>I never had to write any of this down in a settings panel. The AI picked it up from watching my patterns and confirming with me when unsure.</p>

      <h2>What else it handles</h2>

      <p>Class booking is just one example. The same memory and action-taking applies to:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Restaurant reservations</strong> — "Book dinner for 2 Friday night, somewhere in Soho, around HK$400/head"</li>
        <li><strong>Flight bookings</strong> — "Find me a flight to Taipei next weekend, direct if possible"</li>
        <li><strong>Doctor appointments</strong> — "Book a check-up with Dr. Chan next week, morning preferred"</li>
        <li><strong>Meeting scheduling</strong> — "Move my Thursday 3pm to Friday morning, the client requested it"</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The pattern is always the same: I describe what I need in plain language, and my AI handles the rest — because it knows me.</p>

      <h2>The point</h2>

      <p>An AI assistant isn't a chatbot that answers questions. It's something that <em>acts on your behalf</em> — with enough context about your life to make good decisions without asking you 20 follow-up questions.</p>

      <p>That's what we set up for people. Not a tool you have to learn — an assistant that learns you.</p>
